&lt;h2 id="introduction"&gt;Introduction
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;There are strong links to the nearby City of Plymouth to be seen along much of Dartmoor, and in few places are they more obvious than this walk. We stride out from a good sized free car park over the open moorland, which is usually well-grazed by Dartmoor Ponies as well as Sheep, and alongside the Drake&amp;rsquo;s Leat which took water from Burrator down to the City. After half a mile, we drop down and join the well surfaced Railway bed, now re-opened as a multi-use path suitable for walkers, cyclists and horse-riders. Our route takes us through the Leighbeer Tunnel, which has been provided with the luxury of some lighting, and through the industrial remains of the old Railway bed before climbing back up to our starting place.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This walk is a mixture of a short area of exposed moor, then more sheltered woodland hillside and includes a stretch along a quiet lane.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="routing-box"&gt;
&lt;div class="routing-header"&gt;
&lt;img src="https://dartmoorwalking.org/icons/signpost.svg"
alt=""
class="routing-icon"&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="routing-content"&gt;
&lt;em&gt;When you&amp;rsquo;re ready to leave, walk Eastwards. Our GPX takes you along the side of the Drake&amp;rsquo;s Leat, but our map cuts this corner slightly, so choose whichever you prefer&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;h2 id="drakes-leat"&gt;Drake&amp;rsquo;s Leat
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://dartmoorwalking.org/p/clearbrook-and-leighbeer/1.jpg"
width="750"
height="563"
srcset="https://dartmoorwalking.org/p/clearbrook-and-leighbeer/1_hu_9f86c1b8f2110221.jpg 480w, https://dartmoorwalking.org/p/clearbrook-and-leighbeer/1_hu_8136170b24ede296.jpg 1024w"
loading="lazy"
alt="Law angle view from inside Drake’s Leat"
class="gallery-image"
data-flex-grow="133"
data-flex-basis="319px"
&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drake&amp;rsquo;s Leat is also known as Plymouth Leat.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It runs 17.5 miles (28.2km) from the River Meavy to Plymouth. Its route was determined by Mr Robert Lampen of St Budeaux who was paid 52s 5d (£2.62) to survey the possible options in 1576. The Corporation of Plymouth secured an Act of Parliament in 1585 to satisfy the desperate demand for fresh water to supply both the City and the Dockyards, although work did not start until 1590 due to costs involved with our war with Spain and the Armada. The bill had the following clauses:
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;To provide a supply of water for naval and merchant shipping.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;To provide water for fire fighting in Plymouth.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;To scour &lt;a class="link" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sutton_Harbour" title="Sutton Harbour"
target="_blank" rel="noopener"
&gt;Sutton Harbour&lt;/a&gt; of silt.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;To improve the poor quality of land on Dartmoor adjacent to the proposed leat. -&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class="link" href="https://dartmoorpreservation.co.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
&gt;Dartmoor Preservation Society&lt;/a&gt; voluntarily contribute o the upkeep and clearance of the leat.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class="link" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drake%27s_Leat" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
&gt;Wikipedia - Drake&amp;rsquo;s Leat&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
